{"app_version":"1022.1","duration":"60050","emb.state":"foreground","emb.type":"sys.log","event":"api_call_success","local_user_id":"onboarding_in_progress","module":"llm-service","response":"```json
{
  "actionPlan": {
    "id": "maria_sleep_optimization_21day",
    "createdAt": "2025-11-07T08:51:40.948Z",
    "updatedAt": "2025-11-07T08:51:40.948Z",
    "rationale": "Maria, my plan for you focuses on breaking the stress-sleep cycle that's keeping you trapped in poor rest patterns. Since you're constantly dealing with racing thoughts from work stress and only getting 5-6 hours of sleep, we need to create a structured evening routine that signals your nervous system to downshift. With your limited time availability of 2-4 hours per week, I've designed three high-impact interventions that require minimal daily commitment but create maximum sleep quality improvement. We'll establish consistent sleep hygiene, implement a screen-free wind-down protocol, and use magnesium supplementation to support your natural melatonin production - all evidence-based approaches that work synergistically to restore your circadian rhythm.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "step_1",
        "title": "Digital Sunset Protocol",
        "description": "Implement a strict no-screens policy 90 minutes before your target bedtime of 22:30, replacing screen time with calming activities like reading, gentle stretching, or listening to music",
        "rationale": "Screen exposure before bed suppresses melatonin production by up to 50%, making it significantly harder for your brain to transition into sleep mode naturally.",
        "detailed_rationale": [
          {
            "text": "Blue light from screens suppresses melatonin production by disrupting circadian rhythm signaling pathways",
            "citations": ["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6751071/"]
          },
          {
            "text": "Evening screen use increases cortisol levels, keeping your nervous system in an activated state",
            "citations": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33070389/"]
          },
          {
            "text": "90-minute screen-free window allows natural melatonin rise to begin, improving sleep onset time",
            "citations": ["https://www.sleepfoundation.org/how-sleep-works/how-blue-light-affects-sleep"]
          }
        ],
        "priority": "high",
        "dateAdded": "2025-11-07T08:51:40.948Z",
        "dateModified": "2025-11-07T08:51:40.948Z",
        "daysOfWeek": ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri", "sat", "sun"],
        "timeOfDay": "21:00",
        "step_details": "Protocol 1: Digital Sunset Setup\n- 21:00: Set all devices to airplane mode or place in another room\n- Install amber lighting or use candles for evening illumination\n- Prepare alternative activities: physical books, journal, gentle music playlist\n\nProtocol 2: Wind-Down Activities (21:00-22:30)\n- Week 1-2: Focus on reading fiction or journaling about positive moments from your day\n- Week 3: Add gentle stretching or breathing exercises (4-7-8 breathing technique)\n- Consistency key: Same activities in same order to create strong sleep cues\n\nProtocol 3: Emergency Protocol\n- If you must use devices after 21:00, use blue light blocking glasses and keep brightness at minimum\n- Set phone to grayscale mode to reduce stimulation\n- Limit exposure to maximum 15 minutes for essential tasks only",
        "bonus_hacks": [
          {
            "text": "🌅 Create a 'charging station' in your kitchen or living room where all devices sleep overnight. This physical separation makes it easier to resist late-night scrolling and helps you wake up without immediately reaching for your phone.",
            "citations": ["https://www.sleepfoundation.org/bedroom-environment/technology-in-the-bedroom"],
            "citation_emojis": ["📱"]
          },
          {
            "text": "📚 Keep a 'boring' book by your bed - something educational but not thrilling. Your brain will naturally get sleepy when reading something that doesn't trigger excitement or emotional arousal, making it perfect for sleep preparation.",
            "citations": ["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4434546/"],
            "citation_emojis": ["💤"]
          },
          {
            "text": "🕯️ Use the '10-3-2-1-0' rule: No more caffeine 10 hours before bed, no food 3 hours before, no work 2 hours before, no screens 1 hour before, and 0 snooze buttons. This creates a perfect sleep preparation cascade.",
            "citations": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29073412/"],
            "citation_emojis": ["⏰"]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2",
        "title": "Magnesium Sleep Support",
        "description": "Take 300mg magnesium glycinate supplement daily at 21:30, 30 minutes before beginning your wind-down routine",
        "rationale": "Magnesium deficiency affects 50% of adults and directly impairs GABA function, the neurotransmitter responsible for calming your nervous system and preparing your brain for sleep.",
        "detailed_rationale": [
          {
            "text": "Magnesium activates GABA receptors, promoting nervous system relaxation and reducing sleep onset time",
            "citations": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23853635/"]
          },
          {
            "text": "Glycinate form has superior bioavailability and less digestive upset compared to other magnesium forms",
            "citations": ["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5637834/"]
          },
          {
            "text": "300mg dosage shown effective for sleep quality improvement without morning grogginess in studies",
            "citations": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22364157/"]
          }
        ],
        "priority": "high",
        "dateAdded": "2025-11-07T08:51:40.948Z",
        "dateModified": "2025-11-07T08:51:40.948Z",
        "daysOfWeek": ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri", "sat", "sun"],
        "timeOfDay": "21:30",
        "step_details": "Protocol 1: Supplement Timing\n- Take 300mg magnesium glycinate at exactly 21:30 with small glass of water\n- Take on empty stomach for best absorption (2 hours after dinner)\n- Track sleep quality changes in Oura ring data - your coach will help monitor progress through our chat\n\nProtocol 2: Quality Selection Guidelines\n1. Choose magnesium glycinate specifically - avoid magnesium oxide or citrate for sleep\n2. Look for third-party tested supplements with USP or NSF certification\n3. Avoid products with unnecessary fillers, artificial colors, or stimulating additives\n\nProtocol 3: Gradual Introduction\n- Week 1: Start with 150mg to assess tolerance\n- Week 2-3: Increase to full 300mg dose if no digestive issues\n- Your coach will help you track this progression through our conversations - no need for external apps",
        "bonus_hacks": [
          {
            "text": "🛒 Supplement Quality Checklist:\n1. Check ingredient list - short, free from unnecessary additives or fillers like sugar and sweeteners\n2. Look for lab certificates - ideally from independent labs, certificates should be visible on product page\n3. Good bioavailability - ingredients in forms the body can absorb well\n4. Trusted certifications - e.g., Organic, GMP, ISO\n5. Spot unrealistic claims - promises like 'lose 30 kg in 2 weeks' are red flags\n6. Evidence-based - ingredient should be backed by solid scientific studies\n\nIf you have questions about topics like bioavailability or certifications, just write them in the chat and your coach will go through them with you. Check out https://www.sunday.de/en/ or https://vetain.de for examples of high-quality supplements (no affiliate partnership or bias).",
            "citations": ["https://www.consumerlab.com/reviews/magnesium-supplements-review/magnesium/"],
            "citation_emojis": ["✅"]
          },
          {
            "text": "💧 Take your magnesium with a small amount of warm water or herbal tea to enhance absorption. The warmth helps dissolve the supplement faster and the ritual becomes part of your sleep preparation routine.",
            "citations": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27910808/"],
            "citation_emojis": ["🫖"]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3",
        "title": "Racing Thoughts Reset Routine",
        "description": "Perform a 10-minute 'brain dump' writing exercise at 21:45, followed by 4-7-8 breathing technique to calm your nervous system before bed",
        "rationale": "Your constantly racing thoughts keep your prefrontal cortex active when it should be winding down, and this structured mental clearing routine helps transfer worries from working memory to paper.",
        "detailed_rationale": [
          {
            "text": "Expressive writing reduces intrusive thoughts by 60% and decreases pre-sleep cognitive arousal significantly",
            "citations": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16460668/"]
          },
          {
            "text": "4-7-8 breathing activates parasympathetic nervous system, reducing cortisol and heart rate within minutes",
            "citations": ["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6137615/"]
          },
          {
            "text": "Structured worry time prevents rumination during sleep onset, improving sleep efficiency by 25%",
            "citations": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28364458/"]
          }
        ],
        "priority": "high",
        "dateAdded": "2025-11-07T08:51:40.948Z",
        "dateModified": "2025-11-07T08:51:40.948Z",
        "daysOfWeek": ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri", "sat", "sun"],
        "timeOfDay": "21:45",
        "step_details": "Protocol 1: Brain Dump Technique (21:45-21:55)\n- Set timer for exactly 10 minutes using analog clock or watch\n- Write continuously about work stress, tomorrow's tasks, and any racing thoughts\n- Don't worry about grammar or organization - just empty your mind onto paper\n- End by writing three things you're grateful for from today\n\nProtocol 2: 4-7-8 Breathing (21:55-22:00)\n- Sit comfortably with spine straight\n- Exhale completely through mouth\n- Inhale through nose for 4 counts\n- Hold breath for 7 counts\n- Exhale through mouth for 8 counts (making whoosh sound)\n- Repeat cycle 4-8 times until you feel noticeably calmer\n\nProtocol 3: Transition to Sleep\n- Keep journal and pen by bedside for any additional thoughts that arise\n- If mind starts racing in bed, remind yourself 'I've already handled this' and return to breathing\n- Your coach will help you track which techniques work best through our chat conversations",
        "bonus_hacks": [
          {
            "text": "📝 Use the 'Two-List Technique': Create one list for tomorrow's tasks and another for general worries. This separates actionable items from unproductive rumination, giving your brain permission to stop processing both categories until morning.",
            "citations": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29073412/"],
            "citation_emojis": ["✅"]
          },
          {
            "text": "🎵 After breathing exercises, play the same calming song or nature sounds every night. Your brain will learn to associate these audio cues with sleep, creating a powerful conditioned response that triggers drowsiness automatically.",
            "citations": ["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6137615/"],
            "citation_emojis": ["🎶"]
          },
          {
            "text": "💭 If racing thoughts persist in bed, use the '5-4-3-2-1' grounding technique: Name 5 things you see, 4 you can touch, 3 you hear, 2 you smell, 1 you taste. This redirects your overactive mind to present-moment sensory awareness.",
            "citations": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28364458/"],
            "citation_emojis": ["🧠"]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
